## CATT checks what a service did, not just what an agent says

Source: Coverage Cat — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50201860
Published: 2026-10-22
Channel: hn

CATT, the Consumer Agent Testing Tool, helps developers test services used by AI assistants. An assistant can claim success even when its tool calls did not produce the requested result. In an October 22 Show HN announcement, its creators describe a test host that approximates assistants such as Muse and Instinct without claiming to reproduce their private implementations.

Developers specify initial conditions, simulated approvals and the service state that counts as success. The tool records calls and responses so teams can compare what happened with what the agent reported. For illustration, a connector test could check whether a requested record actually exists after an assistant says it created one. That is a hypothetical test, not a reported production incident.

Runs can be local or part of continuous integration, with or without a language model. Assistant-specific assumptions are labelled documented, unofficial, assumed or unknown. Passing these surrogate tests is therefore evidence about the configured scenario, not certification of compatibility with a hosted assistant.

Why it matters: Connector developers should assert service outcomes and review simulator assumptions before trusting a passing test.

