## Langfuse exports the origins of evaluation scores

Source: Langfuse — https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/releases/tag/v4.68.0
Published: 2026-10-22
Channel: github

Langfuse records AI application runs and the evaluation scores attached to them. Exporting a score without its origins can make later analysis difficult: a warehouse may show a low rating without enough information to identify the evaluator or review process behind it. Version 4.68.0 adds optional field groups for score metadata and provenance, meaning information about where a score came from.

For illustration, an evaluation owner could export scores together with evaluator and execution-trace identifiers, then investigate why one batch received different ratings. The new provenance group includes references to the author, configuration, review queue, evaluator, evaluation rule and execution trace. The metadata group is separate. These are exported references, not a claim that the export independently explains or validates a score.

Both groups are opt-in, so existing export selections do not silently gain columns or larger files. They apply to standard and Parquet exports. The linked implementation also warns about version skew: older workers or web interfaces may not understand saved selections containing these groups. Coordinate component upgrades before enabling them.

Why it matters: Evaluation and data-platform owners should enable provenance deliberately and verify that downstream joins and deployed workers understand the new fields.

