## XTrace separates coding-agent activity from estimated time and spend

Source: XTrace — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50197281
Published: 2026-10-22
Channel: hn

XTrace is an open-source macOS app for understanding how coding agents use time and resources. Its October 22 Show HN introduction points to a dashboard built from Claude Code, Codex and Cursor histories already on the machine. The submitter’s follow-up identifies the team’s work on time comparisons, usage forecasts and customizable scoring skills.

The repository distinguishes agent activity from estimated human time, and estimates spending using published API prices rather than subscription bills. It also exposes session timelines and account-usage pacing. For illustration, a developer could compare similar coding sessions before and after changing a workflow, then inspect which sessions consumed more activity or estimated spend. That comparison alone would not show that more useful work was delivered.

The project explicitly cautions against treating extra agent hours as proof of productivity. Missing measurements appear as a dash rather than zero. Its usage forecast uses linear extrapolation, so changing workloads can change the result. These are first-party descriptions, not independently validated savings or productivity measurements.

Why it matters: Coding-agent users should compare similar tasks and review output quality alongside XTrace’s estimates.

